<title>USA: U.S. April import prices fell 0.9 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. April import prices fell 0.9 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-05-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor Department report of</p>
<p>U.S. import and export price changes by percent (unadjusted).</p>
<p>    -			    Apr    Mar-Rev from  Apr 97/96</p>
<p> ALL IMPORTS		  -0.9   -1.2   -1.4     -3.2</p>
<p> -Petroleum		   -3.8   -9.6  -10.9    -11.2</p>
<p> -Nonpetroleum		-0.6   -0.3   -0.3     -2.5</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	 -1.6    3.0    2.8     -0.6</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies     -1.6   -4.2   -4.7     -5.2</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -1.1   -0.2   -0.3     -5.7</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts    -0.2    0.1   UNCH     UNCH</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos  UNCH   -0.6   -0.6     -0.9</p>
<p>Percent changes, not seasonally adjusted --</p>
<p> ALL EXPORTS		  -0.6   UNCH   UNCH     -2.2</p>
<p> -Agricultural		-6.7    1.4    1.4    -16.5</p>
<p> -Non-Agricultural	   0.1   -0.1   -0.1     -0.1</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	 -7.2    1.5    1.5    -18.0</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies     UNCH   UNCH    0.1     -0.2</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     UNCH   -0.1   -0.3     -0.5</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts     0.3    0.2    0.2	1.3</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos   0.1   -0.3   -0.4	0.4</p>
<p>The department reported April price indexes (1995 base year equals 100) of 98.6 for imports, and 99.0 for exports.</p>
<p>The 0.9 percent decline in April import prices was the fourth consecutive monthly decrease. The 0.6 percent drop in non-petroleum import prices last month was the 13th decrease in the last 15 months, the department said.</p>
<p>The 6.7 percent drop in agricultural export prices last month was the largest decrease since last September and pulled down overall export prices, the department said. It attributed the drop in agricultural export prices to falling prices for grains and oilseeds, including a 19.6 percent drop in soybeans, a 19.3 percent drop in corn and a 13.9 percent drop in wheat.</p>
</text>
